<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Khasa Malla kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1100 CE – 1400 CE (approx.) · capital Sinja</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Himalayan kingdom of the Khasa people that ruled western Nepal and parts of western Tibet from Sinja, with a court that patronised both Buddhism and Hinduism.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Khasa Mallas emerged in the Karnali basin of western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a> around the 12th century. Under Nagaraja they made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinja">Sinja</a>, in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-jumla">Jumla</a> valley, their capital and expanded north into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/guge">Guge</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/purang">Purang</a> regions of western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> and south and east across the western Himalayan foothills.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Inscriptions and stone pillars, some in early Nepali written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanagari">Devanagari</a>, record the kings and their donations. Ripumalla and Prithvimalla were among the most powerful rulers; Ripumalla left an inscription on the Ashokan pillar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a> in 1312. The court supported Buddhist monasteries in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> shrines in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Nepal</a>, and the kingdom grew rich on trans-Himalayan trade in salt, wool and grain. The Dullu inscription of Prithvimalla is a key source for its history.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the late 14th century the kingdom fragmented into small principalities later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baise-rajya">Baise Rajya</a>, the twenty-two kingdoms of the Karnali region, among them the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-jumla">Kingdom of Jumla</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-doti">Kingdom of Doti</a>. The language of its inscriptions is an ancestor of modern Nepali.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khasa-malla-kingdom.jpg" alt="Khasa Malla kingdom" width="320" height="319"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ripu_Malla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1100 CE – 1400 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sinja</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nagaraja, Ripumalla, Prithvimalla</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Nepali (Khas)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>, <a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khasa kingdom, Kingdom of Sinja, Malla kingdom of Jumla</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1250&amp;focus=khasa-malla-kingdom" aria-label="Khasa Malla kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6811 1122 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khasa Malla kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7131 1378L7142 1363"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7142 1363h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7131 1378h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7131 1378h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1250&amp;focus=khasa-malla-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
